nging commands (ufw configuration/enable, and the reboot) tie directly to the task's material outcomes or the required restart-evidence protocol; no unrelated or abandoned mutations were found in the executor timeline. However, the fixed after-executor snapshot — captured after all executor commands finished — shows ssh.service and ssh.socket newly in the FAILED state (absent from the before-prepare baseline) and no port 22 socket listening at all, whereas the executor's own last probe 11 seconds earlier showed SSH reachable. This is either a genuine post-task regression left unresolved (the executor never re-checked or remediated it) or a transient/flaky observation, but either way it was not detected, explained, or fixed by the executor, and it directly concerns the very service (SSH) the task required to remain reachable. This lowers the hygiene score even though no destructive or unnecessary mutation was identified in the command trace itself.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"global:after_executor:node1:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets\",\"global:before_prepare:node1:failed-services\",\"cmd-595f99c2429d4f89\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"The ufw firewall was correctly configured on node1 (default deny incoming, allow OpenSSH and 8080/tcp) and enforcement was directly verified via iptables and a rejected probe to an unrelated port, both before and after a full reboot — satisfying the core task contract. However, the independent, fixed after-executor lifecycle snapshot taken moments after the executor's own final verification shows ssh.service and ssh.socket in a FAILED state with no port 22 listener, contradicting the executor's claim that SSH remained reachable post-restart. This unresolved contradiction is the main open issue and prevents full confidence in the persistence/recovery outcome, even though the firewall rule configuration itself is well evidenced.\",\"limitations\":[\"The fixed after-executor snapshot (ssh.service/ssh.socket FAILED,